WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.379
INTRO:
With the rise of affordable streaming services

00:00:02.379 --> 00:00:07.460
like Netflix, Disney+, and Hulu, we’ve seen
a significant reduction in piracy over the

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:08.910
past several years.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:13.349
But, for those that still engage in piracy,
there’s no doubt that torrenting is the

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:15.669
key that makes it all possible.

00:00:15.669 --> 00:00:19.470
If you’re not familiar with torrenting,
it’s basically a file distribution system

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.949
that makes it extremely difficult to shut
down piracy.

00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:27.190
Instead of downloading a file from a central
server, with torrents, you download the file

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:31.140
from thousands of other people who have already
downloaded the file themselves.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.329
So, piracy sites like The Pirate Bay and KickassTorrents
don’t actually have to host the files themselves.

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:40.579
They just have to host the torrenting links
that connect pirates from around the world.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:45.430
So, if prosecutors takedown a certain piracy
website, the hosts of the website just have

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:49.500
to upload the links to a new website which
is no more than a few gigabytes.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.859
In other words, they don’t have to move
the exabytes of data associated with the links

00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:55.170
themselves.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:59.620
This ease of moving websites has made it impossible
to shut down large websites like The Pirate

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.760
Bay, as they can just constantly move websites
thanks to torrents.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.200
As you would guess, prosecutors and copyright
holders aren’t exactly big fans of the man

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.390
who invented torrents: Bram Cohen, but at
the same time, they can’t do anything about

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:14.390
it.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:19.750
So, here’s the story of Bram Cohen and how
he accidentally invented the soul of piracy.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:25.660
BRAM COHEN:
Taking a look back, Bram was born on October

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.810
12, 1975, in Manhattan, New York.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:33.220
His father was a computer scientist and his
mother was a school teacher, so it looks like

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.030
the family didn’t have too much trouble
financially.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.930
From a very early age, it was clear that Bram
wasn’t like most kids.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.940
He had rather mature interests and excelled
at STEM-related competitions.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.920
For example, when he was just 5 years old,
he was trying to learn the programming language

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:48.920
BASIC.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.880
And let’s not forget, when Bram was 5, it
was 1980.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.159
Even today, a 5-year-old learning computer
science would be quite impressive, but given

00:01:57.159 --> 00:02:01.630
how much exposure children have to technology
today, it would make a lot more sense.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:05.810
Back in 1980, the average person didn’t
even know why they needed a computer in the

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.049
first place.

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:10.970
Aside from an interest in programming, Bram
participated in several competitions such

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.110
as the USA Math Olympiad.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.720
Despite this inclination towards academics,
it doesn’t seem like Bram was that sold

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.010
on the idea of school.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:21.200
He didn’t stack on degree after degree like
many other geniuses.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.970
Instead, he attended the University of Buffalo,
and as soon as he started getting job offers,

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:26.720
he left and never looked back.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.140
Bram’s career throughout the 1990s wasn’t
that stable though.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:34.330
It’s not clear if Bram couldn’t qualify
for a job at a big tech company like Microsoft

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:39.110
or Cisco or if he was trying to hit it big
with a startup, but either way, this forced

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:42.770
him to constantly switch jobs as each startup
he worked for went under.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:46.700
While the startup world wasn’t too kind
to Bram, it did provide him with unique concepts

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.311
and ideas, and this was especially true with
his last job at MojoNation.

00:02:50.311 --> 00:02:55.730
MojoNation was trying to create a decentralized
method for safely storing and distributing

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.230
confidential files.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:01.200
The idea was that encrypted chunks of the
original file could be spread across multiple

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.200
computers.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:06.100
This way, to download the entire file, you
would need cooperation from all of the computers.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.120
So, a hacker couldn’t just get access to
one of these computers and steal the entire

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.120
file.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.610
It’s not clear if this decentralized encryption
method ever caught on, but this idea did inspire

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:19.390
Bram who thought that it was perfect for file
sharing.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:24.930
BITTORRENT:
In April of 2001, Bram went ahead and quit

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:28.570
MojoNation determined to bring his file-sharing
idea to life.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:32.840
Over the next year, he programmed the first
BitTorrent client which ran on Python.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:37.730
Soon enough, Bram completed a beta version
of the software that worked alright, but Bram

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.730
was well aware that the key to success was
not the program itself but getting people

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:42.730
to see it.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:46.700
So, he worked with his roommate, Len Sassaman,
to create a tech conference that you might’ve

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.450
heard of: CodeCon.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:53.510
Since then, even people as notable as Sergey
Brin and Elon Musk have presented at CodeCon.

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.870
But Bram and BitTorrent were the stars of
the first CodeCon.

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:00.890
This presentation allowed Bram to create some
hype within the tech world, but this was not

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:03.080
exactly his target audience per se.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:05.880
So, Bram developed a new idea to attract users.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:10.560
In the summer of 2002, Bram went out and got
some adult films and offered them for free

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.950
on BitTorrent.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:16.320
This strategy was surprisingly effective,
but Bram didn’t want to be known as an adult

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.450
film distributor.

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:21.620
So after he built up a solid base of users,
he stopped seeding these films.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:25.880
By this point though, many BitTorrent users
started to realize the true potential of the

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:26.880
program.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.950
Nowadays we have extremely reliable cloud
storage platforms like Google Drive and Mega

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:34.720
which allow users to download gigabytes worth
of files in a matter of minutes.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.880
But, back in the early 2000s, moving around
such large files was not only difficult but

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.740
expensive due to the storage and bandwidth
requirements.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.470
So, no one wanted to host such files.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:48.160
With BitTorrent though, no single person had
to host the files.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.810
Instead, hundreds or even thousands of users
could host them.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:55.920
And given that average people aren’t charged
for their bandwidth usage, this was basically

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.700
free for most hosts.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:01.160
Considering these perks, I don’t think you’d
be surprised to hear that BitTorrent became

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.450
the go-to place to share and download large
files.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:08.670
And naturally, it’s not like people needed
BitTorrent to share 4 GB word documents or

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:09.980
excel spreadsheets.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:14.120
No, the most common files that were large
enough to require BitTorrent were movies,

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.780
games, and software.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:19.560
And as users started to realize how easily
such files could be shared using BitTorrent,

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.500
the program quickly became the sole of piracy.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:28.110
STANCE ON PIRACY:
Given BitTorrent’s major role within piracy,

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.660
it’s not surprising that BitTorrent was
a massive target for prosecutors.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:36.680
But the problem is that they couldn’t really
charge BitTorrent or Bram of copyright infringement.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.870
Bram has made it clear that he didn’t create
BitTorrent for piracy.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:45.180
His main goal was simply to allow users to
share and download large files cheaper, faster,

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.800
and more efficiently than ever before.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:51.760
And it just so happens that the community
started using it for illicit activities.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.080
So, given that there was no conspiracy to
commit copyright infringement, prosecutors

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.080
can’t do anything.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:01.270
It would be like the FBI taking action against
Facebook because scammers are using WhatsApp

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:03.200
to conduct Bitcoin scams.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:07.600
With that being said though, piracy is a way
bigger part of BitTorrent than Bitcoin scams

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:08.630
are of WhatsApp.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:12.639
So, prosecutors were constantly looking for
ways to build a case against BitTorrent.

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:17.220
But, Bram was well aware of this and he was
sure to play it safe legally.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.730
He didn’t pirate himself, he didn’t encourage
others to pirate, and he constantly tried

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:23.850
to make BitTorrent more applicable to other
use cases as well.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:27.389
So, this was Bram’s legal stance on the
piracy issue.

00:06:27.389 --> 00:06:30.220
His moral stance, however, is a lot different.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:33.950
Bram believes that copyright holders need
to get a life a stop going after every medium

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:36.380
that inadvertently enables piracy.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:40.380
He argues that if copyrighted content was
offered in an affordable and easily accessible

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.169
way, people would have no reason to pirate
content.

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:46.720
And this does make sense given what happened
to the music industry.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.630
Back in the 2000s, it was extremely common
to pirate music to put on MP3 players and

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:51.630
iPods.

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:55.760
But, with the explosive growth of services
like YouTube, Spotify, and Apple Music, music

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.690
piracy has significantly declined.

00:06:57.690 --> 00:07:01.760
I mean, why would you want to go onto sketchy
websites and risk getting a virus when you

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:06.180
can legally listen to any song on YouTube
or Spotify for free with ads or for a reasonable

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:07.280
fee without ads.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:11.850
So, I do think Bram’s argument makes quite
a bit of sense, but this didn’t stop copyright

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:15.300
holders from trying to fight back anyway.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:19.129
RETALIATION:
Since copyright holders couldn’t directly

00:07:19.129 --> 00:07:22.930
shut down or convict BitTorrent, they decided
to go after the ones that were sharing the

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:23.930
content themselves.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:28.300
Initially, BitTorrent was slightly involved
in this given that there were links to copyrighted

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:30.330
material on BitTorrent’s official website.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:35.560
But, in 2005, Bram made a deal with 7 American
studios that he would remove all piracy-related

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:37.310
links from the website.

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:40.310
This led to the rise of sites like The Pirate
Bay and KickassTorrents.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:44.571
I have videos about both these sites if you’re
interested, but in this video, we’ll be

00:07:44.571 --> 00:07:46.670
focusing on seeders.

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:49.760
Seeders are basically the thousands of hosts
around the world that are making the content

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.330
available to pirate.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:55.090
And since copyright holders couldn’t take
down BitTorrent, they decided to take down

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:56.090
seeders.

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:59.590
Copyright holders started finding the IP addresses
of seeders and contacting their internet service

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:01.570
providers to cut their internet.

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:05.910
Considering these consequences, you might
be wondering, why would anybody seed content?

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:10.510
Well, within the piracy community, it’s
common courtesy to seed for as long as you

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:11.510
downloaded.

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:14.770
But, even if you don’t seed out of free
will, you’ll automatically be seeding as

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:16.470
you’re downloading the content itself.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:21.290
For example, if you’re pirating Avengers
End Game and your progress bar is at 80%,

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:25.400
this means that you’ve already downloaded
the first 80% which you could be seeding as

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.550
you’re downloading the last 20%.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:31.010
Copyright holders have actually gotten really
good at identifying seeders and cutting their

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:36.300
internet which has forced most pirates to
start using VPNs which is quite ironic.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:39.880
If you’re pirating any amount of substantial
content, you’re gonna have to get a paid

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:40.880
VPN.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:44.820
ExpressVPN’s cheapest option itself costs
$100 per year.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:48.110
Meanwhile, Disney+ only costs $80 per year.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:52.310
So, clearly, there’s some sort of disparity
going on here if pirates are more willing

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:56.260
to pay for a VPN than a streaming service
as Bram suggested.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:00.890
Nonetheless, copyright holders have continued
to spend a lot of effort in taking down seeders.

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:05.820
LIFE AFTER BITTORRENT:
For many founders within the piracy community,

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:09.320
they have no life after founding the piracy
website.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:12.730
Most of them have several charges against
them, they owe millions to copyright holders,

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:14.890
and/or they’re fugitives on the run.

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:19.170
But, given that Bram was always able to stay
on the legal side of the law, there’s nothing

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:22.830
restricting him, and he used this freedom
to move past BitTorrent.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:27.460
In 2017, Bram founded a cryptocurrency company
called Chia Network.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:31.580
One of the main goals of the company was to
move cryptocurrency away from proof of work

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:33.300
and onto proof of stake.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:37.690
If you’re not sure what that means, it basically
means eliminating the need for miners.

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:42.000
Chia launched their coin in May of 2021, and
it hasn’t been doing so hot.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:45.450
In fact, it’s down 95% from its peak.

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:49.680
But it should be noted that the coin launched
at the peak of the altcoin bull market, so

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:53.080
overall crypto conditions haven’t been too
kind to Chia.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:56.890
With that being said though, given the existence
of much more popular projects with similar

00:09:56.890 --> 00:10:01.000
goals like Solana and Cardano, it’s not
clear if Chia will ever take off.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.000
I don’t think Bram minds too much though
given that he’s estimated to be worth $15

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:03.000
million.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:05.550
At the end of the day, Bram was the mastermind
behind the file-sharing protocol that has

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:07.770
made piracy what it is today.

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:11.500
Despite this status within the piracy community,
prosecutors can’t really do anything to

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:15.450
Bram given that he didn’t create BitTorrent
with the intention of pirating nor does he

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.400
encourage or promote piracy.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:21.400
This has allowed him to live a rather free
life relative to other piracy kings, but none

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:25.830
of his other projects thus far have achieved
similar levels of success to BitTorrent.

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:29.920
And that’s the story of the torrenting king
that the FBI can’t do anything about.

